%%
.1
Menu Bar 
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The menu bar provides the means for all EZ-ICE windows to be chosen and 
subsequently displayed.  At the top of the screen are a list of categories.
Upon selecting one of the categories at the top of screen, a drop down sub-menu 
containing choices relevant to the selected category will appear.  These choices
typically specify windows to open or actions to occur.  Selecting one of these
options will either open the chosen window or cause the EZ-ICE to take the 
given action. 

Note: The <ESC> key will cancel a menu selection.  Press <ESC> twice if a
sub-menu is displayed, but just press <ESC> once if only the menu bar itself is
selected.  

These are the categories of the menu bar:

 @180[File]  @181[Register]  @182[Memory]  @183[Stacks]  @184[Commands]  @185[Execution]  @186[Display]  @187[Misc]  @179[Help]  @188[Quit]
 
.2
Exit
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The Exit function provides a means to leave the EZ-ICE and return to the
operating system.  The current display configuration, this includes window
sizes, positions, colors and floating point display attributes are written out
to the last window file that was loaded.  If no window file was loaded, the
configuration is saved to a file which the EZ-ICE will read in by default.

A pop-up window appears prompting the user for confirmation before the EZ-ICE
exits. 

Exit can also be called from the @77[Command Window] by typing "exit".
No confirmation is required when exiting from the Command Window.

See also @3[Quit].

.3
Quit
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The Quit function provides a means to leave the EZ-ICE and return to the
operating system.  No display configuration data is saved.

A pop-up window appears prompting the user for confirmation before the EZ-ICE
exits. 

Quit can also be called from the @77[Command Window] by typing "quit".
No confirmation is required when quitting from the Command Window.

See also @2[Exit].

.4
Abort 
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The Abort function actually provides several functions depending upon the
current context.  It is usually invoked by pressing the <ESC> key.

   - When invoked from the @176[help system], this function will return the user to 
the EZ-ICE.

   - When invoked from the active window, this function will close the active
window.

   - When invoked from the @1[menu bar], this function will cancel any selections
made from the menu.

   - When invoked from a pop-up, this function will close the pop-up and cancel
any actions incurred by the pop-up.

.5
Next Window
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function allows the user to move between windows.  This function is invoked
by the @178[<F2>] key.  

.6
Redraw Display
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function simply redraws the entire screen.  This function is invoked from
the @1[menu bar].   

.7
Center Windows
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will center all open windows to the middle of the screen.  It is 
typically used when some window(s) has been moved off the screen.  This function
is invoked from the @1[menu bar]. 

.8
List Windows
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will produce a pop-up window containing a list of all open
windows.  The user can select from the list a window to transfer control 
into or simply remain in the current window.

This function can be invoked from the @1[menu bar] or by pressing @178[<Ctrl-L>].

.9
Move Window Up
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will move the current window, if that window is a movable window,
toward the top of the screen.  This function can be invoked by pressing the
@178[<F5>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to move windows by clicking and dragging the window
border to the new location.

.10
Move Window Down
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will move the current window, if that window is a movable window,
toward the bottom of the screen.  This function can be invoked by pressing
the @178[<F6>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to move windows by clicking and dragging the window
border to the new location.

.11
Move Window Left
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will move the current window, if that window is a movable window,
toward the left side of the screen.  This function can be invoked by pressing
the @178[<F7>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to move windows by clicking and dragging the window
border to the new location.

.12
Move Window Right
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will move the current window, if that window is a movable window,
toward the right side of the screen.  This function can be invoked by pressing
the @178[<F8>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to move windows by clicking and dragging the window
border to the new location.

.14
Make Window Taller
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will vertically increase the size of the current window, if that
window is a sizeable window.  This function can be invoked by pressing the
@178[<Shift-F5>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to size windows by clicking and dragging a corner
of the window border.

.15
Make Window Shorter
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will vertically decrease the size of the current window, if that
window is a sizeable window.  This function can be invoked by pressing the
@178[<Shift-F6>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to size windows by clicking and dragging a corner
of the window border.

.16
Make Window Wider
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will horizontally increase the size of the current window, if that
window is a sizeable window.  This function can be invoked by pressing the
@178[<Shift-F7>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to size windows by clicking and dragging a corner
of the window border.

.17
Make Window Narrower
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will horizontally decrease the size of the current window, if that
window is a sizeable window.  This function can be invoked by pressing the
@178[<Shift-F8>] key.  

The mouse can also be used to size windows by clicking and dragging a corner
of the window border.

.18
Toggle Window Format
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will toggle the current display format of the current window, 
assuming the window is capable of supporting multiple formats.  For example,
when the current window is a Program Memory window, this function will
change the display format from it's current format to either hexadecimal, 
decimal, or disassembled.  

This function is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @178[<Ctrl-T>] key.

.19
Video Mode
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function allows the user to change the video mode of the display.  The
EZ-ICE supports 3 video modes, all of which are text modes:

                 - 80 Columns x 25 Rows (default)
                 - 80 Columns x 43 Rows (EGA and VGA only)
                 - 80 Columns x 50 Rows (VGA only)

The EZ-ICE first determines which modes are supported on the hardware 
platform and then pops up a selection menu containing a list of choices based
upon the hardware configuration.  The video mode is saved in and loaded from
the the window file.  This function is invoked from the the @1[menu bar]. 

See also @99[Save Window File] and @98[Load Window File].

(NOTE: This feature is supported on PC platforms only.)

.20
About EZ-ICE...
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function displays information about the EZ-ICE.  This function is
invoked from the the @1[menu bar]. 


.21
DAG Registers
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

These are the Data Address Generator  (DAG) registers.  The DAGs are used to
generate memory addresses.  There are 4 sets of 14 bit registers in each DAG.

                    Index Registers:   I0 thru I3 DAG1  I4 thru I7 DAG2
                    Modify Registers:  M0 thru M3 DAG1  M4 thru M7 DAG2
                    Length Registers:  L0 thru L3 DAG1  L4 thru L7 DAG2

The DAG registers can be displayed in hexadecimal and decimal formats. 
Note that in the decimal display format the I and L registers are
displayed as 14 bit unsigned values whereas the M registers are displayed in 14
bit signed two's complement format.  The current format can be changed by 
pressing @18[<Ctrl-T>].

.23
Computational Registers
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This is the display of the computational registers.  These registers are the
foreground or background ALU and MAC registers. The selection of primary and
secondary sets is made via the @33[MSTAT] register.

The registers can be displayed in hexadecimal, decimal 
and 1.15 fixed point formats.
The current format can be changed by pressing @18[<Ctrl-T>].

.29
Memory Mapped Control Registers
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The control registers consist of the wait state registers, (PWAIT and DWAIT0
thru DWAIT4), the timer control registers ,(timer scale, timer period, and
timer count), the sport 0 and sport 1 enables, and the boot control 
registers (boot force, boot page, boot wait, and boot mode for 2111).

These registers use a total of 5 16-bit data words in the memory-mapped
registers.

Sport enable/configure, boot registers, and PWAIT are at dm[0x3fff].

DWAIT0-4 are at dm[0x3fe].

TPERIOD is at dm[0x3ffd], TCOUNT is at dm[0x3ffc], and TSCALE is at dm[0x3ffb].

.31
Program Control Registers
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The program control registers display the current values of the program
counter (PC), and the counter register (CNTR).  Both values can be set
from the window or from the @77[Command Window].

.33
Status Registers
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The arithmetic status register (ASTAT), stack status register (SSTAT),
and the mode status register (MSTAT) are broken down into their bit
fields in this window.  All three registers bit fields are arranged in
ascending order, with the LSB at the top, and the MSB at the bottom.
ASTAT and MSTAT are read/write.  SSTAT is read-only.  These values can
also be read/set from the @77[Command Window].

The ASTAT information is generated by the computational units.  The bits
in ASTAT are defined as follows:

   Bit   Name	 Definition
    0     AZ      ALU result zero or floating point underflow
    1     AV      ALU overflow
    2     AN      ALU result negative
    3     AC      ALU fixed point carry
    4     AS      ALU X input sign 
    5     AQ      ALU quotient sign
    6     MV      MAC overflow
    7     SS      Shifter input sign

@33[more]

The bits in the SSTAT register are defined as follows:

   Bit    Name    Definition
    0     PC_E    PC stack empty        see also @106[PC stack]
    1     PC_O    PC stack overflow     see also @106[PC stack]
    2     CS_E    Count stack empty     see also @108[Loop stack]
    3     CS_O    Count stack overflow  see also @108[Loop Stack]
    4     SS_E    Status stack empty    see also @107[Status stack]
    5     SS_O    Status stack overflow see also @107[Status stack]
    6     LS_E    Loop stack empty      see also @108[Loop stack]
    7     LS_O    Loop stack overflow   see also @108[Loop stack]

The bits in the MSTAT register are defined as follows:

   Bit    Name    Definition
    0     RBANK   0 primary, 1 secondary registers
    1     BIT_R   1 = bit reverse operation
    2     ALUOV   ALU overflow latch mode
    3     ARSAT   AR saturation mode
    4     MACRP   MAC result placement mode 
    5     T_ENA   Timer enable 
    6     G_ENA   Go Mode enabled

.35
Interrupt Control Registers
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The Interrupt Control Registers consist of the interrupt mask register
(IMASK), and the interrupt control register (ICNTL). Both these
registers are read/write.  Also present is the IFC register which is
a write-only register.  Its display will always read 000.

The bits in the IMASK register are defined for the 2101 as follows:
   Bit      Definition
    0       Timer interrupt enable
    1       SPORT 1 receive or IRQ0 interrupt enable
    2       SPORT 1 transmit or IRQ1 interrupt enable
    3       SPORT 0 receive interrupt enable (Must be set to 0 for 2105)
    4       SPORT 0 transmit interrupt enable (Must be set to 0 for 2105)
    5       IRQ2 interrupt enable

The bits in the ICNTL register are defined as follows:
   Bit      Definition
    0       IRQ0 sensitivity 0 = level , 1 = edge
    1       IRQ1 sensitivity 0 = level , 1 = edge
    2       IRQ2 sensitivity 0 = level , 1 = edge
    3       IRQ3 sensitivity (always 0 for 2101)
    4       Interrupt Nesting mode

The bits in the IFC register are defined as follows:
   Bit      Definition
    0       Timer clear
    1       SPORT 1 receive or IRQ0 clear
    2       SPORT 1 transmit or IRQ1 clear
    3       SPORT 0 receive clear (must be set to 0 for 2105)
    4       SPORT 0 transmit clear (must be set to 0 for 2105)
    5       IRQ2 clear
    6       Timer force
    7       SPORT 1 receive or IRQ0 force
    8       SPORT 1 transmit or IRQ1 force
    9       SPORT 0 receive force (must be set to 0 for 2105)
   10       SPORT 0 transmit force (must be set to 0 for 2105)
   11       IRQ2 force

.36
PX Register
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The PX register is used for 24 bit data transfers. Essentially, the
LSB of data from a read of PM data is loaded into PX. The LSB of data
when writing to PM data memory is written from the PX register.

.38
Sport Control Registers
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The SPORT control registers are broken out by SPORT in these windows.
(0,1) refers to SPORT 0 or SPORT 1.
The following chart describes these registers:

Name                  Description
SLEN(0,1)             serial length of data
DTYPE(0,1)            data type (format of the serial data)
ISCLK(0,1)            sclk required flag
IRFS(0,1)             internal receive frame synch 
ITFS(0,1)             internal transmit frame synch 
RFSR(0,1)             receive frame synch required flag
TFSR(0,1)             transmit frame synch required flag
RFSW(0,1)             receive frame synch width
TFSW(0,1)             transmit frame synch width
SCLKDIV(0,1)          sclk divisor (use to set up frequency)
RFSDIV(0,1)           receive frame synch divisor 
@38[more]
RBUF(0,1)             receive auto-buffering enable
TBUF(0,1)             transmit auto-buffering enable
RIREG(0,1)            receive auto-buffering I register
TIREG(0,1)            transmit auto-buffering I register
RMREG(0,1)            receive auto-buffering M register
TMREG(0,1)            transmit auto-buffering M register
MCRE0                 multichannel receive word zero enables
MCRE1                 multichannel receive word one enables
MCTE0                 multichannel transmit word zero enables
MCTE1                 multichannel transmit word one enables
MCE0                  multichannel enable
MCEFD                 multichannel frame delay
RX(0,1)               receive register          
TX(0,1)               transmit register          
@38[first page]

.43
Hip Registers
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The hip register window shows the values currently stored in the hip data
registers HDR0-5.  The registers are memory mapped to DM 0x3fe0 - 0x3fe5 
and are read/writable.  Also shown are the current values of HSR6 & HSR7.
These registers indicate reads & writes of the HDR registers by the dsp 
and an external host.  Bits 0-5 of HSR6 are set when an external host writes
to HDR 0-5 respectively.  Bits 8-13 are set when the dsp writes to the HDRs.
When the dsp reads an HDR, its corresponding bit is cleared.  Bits 0-5 of
HSR7 mirror the upper half of HSR6 (dsp writes) so that they can be read by 
an 8 bit host.  Bit 6 is the software reset bit.  If written by the dsp, 
reset occurs immediately, otherwise the reset happens when the write is
synchronized to the dsp's system clock.  _RESET_ is held for 5 cycles then
released.  Bit 7 of HSR7 is the overwrite bit, and is the only part of the
HSRs that can be written to from the interface.  There is a separate field
in the hip register window dedicated to the overwrite bit.  The overwrite 
bit determines how the dsp responds to an external host trying to write to
an HDR before it has been read by the dsp.  Consult the ADSP-2100 Family
Users Manual for more information on overwrite mode.


.52
Program Memory
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

Program memory (PM) is 24 bits wide and spans from address 0 through 0x3fff.
The display contains the memory map access for the location, address, data (in
whatever format), and symbol name, if any. PM can be displayed in hexadecimal,
decimal, and disassembled formats. 

In the disassembled format window, highlighted address fields indicate
the presence of a @169[Breakpoint].  Also note
that the instruction pointed to by the @31[Program Counter] 
(PC) is highlighted, as well as the line currently selected for 
input (if window is active).

The following @178[special keys] are active for PM windows:
        @60[<Ctrl-D>] Dump Memory
        @61[<Ctrl-M>] Load Memory
        @62[<Ctrl-F>] Fill Memory
        @63[<Ctrl-G>] Go To Memory
        @18[<Ctrl-T>] Toggles Window Display Format

.56
Data Memory
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

Data memory (DM) is 16 bits wide and spans from address 0 through
0x3fff.  The display contains the memory map access for the location,
address, data (in whatever format), and symbol name, if any.  DM can
be displayed in hexadecimal, and decimal formats.  The memory-mapped
control registers occupy DM locations 0x3c00 - 0x3fff, and these
locations are not accessible from the DM memory window.

The following @178[special keys] are active for DM windows:

        @60[<Ctrl-D>] Dump Memory
        @61[<Ctrl-M>] Load Memory
        @62[<Ctrl-F>] Fill Memory
        @63[<Ctrl-G>] Go To Memory
        @18[<Ctrl-T>] Toggles Window Display Format

.60
Dump Memory
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to dump memory data to a file.  You are prompted
for an address range and file name.  The data is dumped in a format
consistent with the format of the window from which it is invoked, i.e.:
hexadecimal, decimal etc..

This function is valid only when applied from a memory, @52[PM] or @56[DM] window.  
It is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @178[<Ctrl-D>] key.

See also @61[Load Memory].
.61
Load Memory
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to load memory data from a file.  You are prompted for
an address range and file name.  The data is loaded, in a format consistent
with the format of the window from which it is invoked, i.e.: hexadecimal,
or decimal, starting at the address supplied.  

This function is valid only when applied from a memory, @52[PM] or @56[DM] window. 
It is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @178[<Ctrl-M>] key.

See also @60[Dump Memory].
.62
Fill Memory
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to fill memory with a given pattern.  You are prompted 
for an address range and pattern which will be replicated in memory.  The
pattern is entered in hexadecimal format.  

This function is valid only when applied from a memory, @52[PM] or @56[DM] window.
It is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @178[<Ctrl-F>] key.
.63
Go To Address
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to go to a particular memory address.  You are prompted 
for an address.  After entering the address, the window is moved to the given
address.  

This function is valid only when applied from a memory, @52[PM] or @56[DM] window. 
It is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @178[<Ctrl-G>] key.

.64
Plot memory
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to plot a section of memory.  A pop-up asks for the
range of memory to be plotted.  A second pop-up asks for the 'stride' or
spacing of locations in the range specified.  The plot's scale is auto-sizing.
When finished viewing, hit 'enter' to return to the EZ-ICE.

It is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar] or via the @178[<Ctrl-P>] key.

.69
Chip Reset 
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

A full chip reset (with boot if MMAP == 0) is invoked here. Breakpoints
are not affected by this action.

N.B.  The EZ-ICE will immediately reset the chip, without confirmation
from the user.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar], or by typing "cr"
in the @77[Command Window].

.70
Emulator Reset
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function causes a reset of the EZ-ICE host.  A chip reset
(with boot if MMAP == 0) is performed.  Breakpoints are all cleared,
all host memory caches are invalidated, and communications with
the EZ-ICE are re-established, with a communications check performed.

A pop-up window will confirm before any action is taken.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].

See also @69[Chip Reset].

.71
Color Display Attributes
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to alter the color attributes of the windowing
system of the EZ-ICE.  A pop-up window is displayed with a list of
window types and areas within those windows on the left hand side and
the corresponding color attributes currently in effect.  Upon choosing
the item for which a change in color is desired, another pop-up window
is displayed which presents a palette of colors.  The current color
attributes for the item which was selected is enclosed by brackets
([]).  Upon choosing a new color selection, the original pop-up is
redisplayed, showing the new color selections.

Selecting the "Accept" option will redisplay the screen with the new
color selections.  The "Cancel" option cancels any new color
selections made.  The "Reset" option resets all colors to the EZ-ICE
defaults.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

Note that color attributes are saved in window files, either upon @2[exiting] the
EZ-ICE or manually saving the @99[window file].  

.77
Command Window
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The Command Window is used to enter EZ-ICE commands. A command history of 20
commands is kept and may be recalled and edited using the arrow keys or the 
mouse. The format for the commands is as follows:

Register Commands

   Function                          Command Syntax

   Set a register to a value         register = expression (ex. ax0=1)
			             R register value (ex. r ax0 1)

   Examine contents of register      ?register (ex. ?ax0)
   (displayed in command output
   window)



@77[more]
Memory Commands

    Set memory to value              FILL addr or range value 
                                     SETUP addr or range value 
                                     (ex. FILL pm(1) 1234)
                                     (ex. FILL pm(1),pm(10) 1234)
                                     (ex. FILL pm(1)/10 1234)
                                     (ex. FILL pm(1)< 'file')
                                     (ex. SETUP pm(1) 1234)
                                     (ex. SETUP pm(1),pm(10) 1234)
                                     (ex. SETUP pm(1)/10 1234)
                                     (ex. SETUP pm(1)< 'file')

    Assemble value                   ASSEMBLE addr instruction 
                                     A addr instruction
                                     (ex. assemble pm(0) jump 10)
                                     (ex. A pm(10) ax0=ar)

   Examine contents of               ?addr (ex. ?dm(3) )
   memory

   Dump contents of                  DUMP addr or range [>'file']
   memory                            D addr or range [>'file']
                                     (ex. D dm[0]/0x100 >'d.dat')

   Load executable file              LOAD 'file'
                                     L 'file'

   Plot memory                       PLOT range increment
                                     PL range increment
                                     (ex. PL pm[0]/0x100 1)
@77[more]
Break/Watch Commands

    Set a breakpoint                 BREAK addr (ex. BREAK pm(10))
                                     B addr (ex. B pm(10))

    Delete a breakpoint              BD addr or number or all
                                     (ex. BD pm(10)))
                                     (ex. BD 1)
                                     (ex. BD all)

    List breakpoints                 B
    to command output window


@77[more]
Execution Commands

    Run                              GO
                                     G
                                     RUN

    Run to an address                GO addr
                                     G addr

    Step                             STEP
                                     S

    Step 'N' times                   STEP number
                                     S number

    Chip reset (boot if MMAP 0)      CR

    Execute a single instruction     EXECUTE instruction
                                     EXE instruction
                                     (ex. EXE ar = ay0 + 1)
@77[more]


Symbols and Alias Commands

    Load a symbol file               LS 'file'
                                     (ex. LS 'test')

    Add a user defined symbol        ADDSYMBOL + 'symbol' addr
                                     ASYM + 'symbol' addr
                                     (ex. ADDSYMBOL + 'ggg' pm[3])

    Set an alias                     ALIAS 'new name' 'command'
                                     (ex. ALIAS 'my_reset' 'cr')

    Delete an alias                  DELETE 'new name'
                                     (ex. DELETE 'my_reset')

    List alias to command            ALIAS
    output window



@77[more]
File Related Commands

    Write a window file              WIN > 'filename'

    Start writing to keystroke       KEYON 'filename'
    file                             KO 'filename'

    Stop writing to keystroke        KEYOFF
    file                             KF

    Read in a keystroke file         < 'filename'

Miscellaneous Commands            

    Quit the EZ-ICE without       QUIT 
