				     VS



@77[first page]

.85
Command Output Window
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The Command Output Window displays information requested from the 
@77[Command Window].
Data is displayed in the same format as selected in the Command Window.
The following data is displayed in the Command Output Window:

   Command                          Output

   ALIAS                            List of current aliases

   BREAK,B                          List of current breakpoints

   CHIPRESET,CR                     Boot Cycles, Boot Page

   D range                          Contents of memory 

   ? expression                     The value of the expression.

.90
Expression Evaluation
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The Expressions Window can display and evaluate expressions while you
perform other debugging actions. The EZ-ICE updates an expression's
value as it changes. The ?+ command (See the @77[Command Window]) adds an expression and
the ?- command deletes an expression. You can also type an expression
directly into the command window. CTRL_D (control D) will delete an
expression.

Values displayed in the Expressions Window are always displayed as 32 bit sign-
extended numbers.

.96
Load Executable File
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to load executable files into the EZ-ICE.  The 
executable file typically defines @52[Program Memory] (PM), or
@56[Data Memory] (DM) and symbolic information.

When prompted for a filename to load, if an extension is given, only
the executable is loaded.  If no extension is given, then the EZ-ICE
looks for a symbol table under the same filename with the extension .sym, 
and loads it if found.

Note that executable files are loaded independently of any memory map
attributes.  

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar], or from the
@77[Command Window] by typing "l 'filename'".

See also @97[Load Symbol File].

.97
Load Symbol Table
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function loads in a symbol table only.  It does not load any code
or perform data memory initialization.  When prompted for a filename,
the .sym extension is optional, it will be automagically appended if
you don't supply it.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar], or from the
@77[Command Window] by typing "ls 'filename'".

See also @96[Load Executable File].

.98
Load Window File
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to load window files into the EZ-ICE.  The
window file defines video mode, color attributes, window types, window
sizes and screen positions.

When a window file is loaded, the current window attributes are replaced with 
those from the file.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also @99[Save Window File].

.99
Save Window File
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to create a window file for the EZ-ICE.  The
window file defines video mode, color attributes, window types, window
sizes and screen positions.

When a window file is created, the current window attributes are
stored in the filename supplied by the user.  Creating a window file
does not affect the current window attributes in any manner.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also @98[load window file].

.100
Start Keystroke Recording
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to create a keystroke file for the EZ-ICE.  A
keystroke file is essentially a log of both keystrokes and mouse
actions that can be created and then "played back".  It is typically
used when a set of actions is, or will be, done repeatedly.  When a
keystroke file is created, all subsequent keystrokes and mouse actions
are recorded to the given file, until the @101[Stop Keystroke Recording] command is
issued.  Later, the file can be "played back" with the @102[Playback Keystroke File] command.

There is something to watch out for when using keystroke files.
Because of the mechanism used to replay keystroke files, the use of
commands that wait for any keystroke, the run command for example,
should be avoided.  What will happen in this case is that immediately
after the run command is issued from the file, the next keystroke from
the file would be read; the EZ-ICE will interpret this as signal to
stop running and would halt the simulation.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also @101[Stop Keystroke Recording] and @102[Playback Keystroke File].
.101
Stop Keystroke Recording
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to stop recording keystrokes to a keystroke
file.  The keystroke file is closed and no further keystrokes or mouse
actions are logged to the file.

There is something to watch out for when using keystroke files.
Because of the mechanism used to replay keystroke files, the use of
commands that wait for any keystroke, the run command for example,
should be avoided.  What will happen in this case is that immediately
after the run command is issued from the file, the next keystroke from
the file would be read; the EZ-ICE will interpret this as signal to
stop running and would halt the simulation.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

See also @100[Start Keystroke Recording] and @102[Playback Keystroke File].
.102
Playback Keystroke File
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to "playback" a keystroke file.  All keystrokes
and mouse actions are repeated in exactly the same order in which the
keystroke file was created.

There is something to watch out for when using keystroke files.
Because of the mechanism used to replay keystroke files, the use of
commands that wait for any keystroke, the run command for example,
should be avoided.  What will happen in this case is that immediately
after the run command is issued from the file, the next keystroke from
the file would be read; the EZ-ICE will interpret this as signal to
stop running and would halt the simulation.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar], or from the 
@77[Command Window] by typing "< 'filename'".

See also @100[Start Keystroke Recording] and @101[Stop Keystroke Recording].

.103
Display File
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to display a text file.  When invoked, the user is 
prompted for a filename.  The file is then loaded into the EZ-ICE and
displayed.  

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  

.104
Operating System Shell
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to create an operating system shell.  

For DOS platforms, the EZ-ICE is suspended and the user is transferred to
an operating system shell.  Typing 'exit' from the shell will return the
user to the EZ-ICE.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar], or from the
@77[Command Window] by typing "sh"

***NOTE: This feature is not available in release 1.0 of the EZ-ICE***

.105
Operating System Shell Command
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to create an operating system shell and execute an
OS command.  

For DOS platforms, the EZ-ICE is suspended and the command is executed 
in  the operating system shell.  When the command is complete, control
transfers back to the EZ-ICE.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar] or
by using the SH 'command' command from the @77[Command Window].

***NOTE: This feature is not available in release 1.0 of the EZ-ICE***

.106
PC Stack
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This is the display of the Program Counter (PC) stack.  The PC stack
is 14 bits wide and 16 locations deep.  Also shown is the number of
valid stack entries.  The stack entries are numbered in the window for
easy reference.  Entry 1 is the top of the stack.  Unused locations
are shown as 3fff.  Valid stack locations are writable, as is the
stack count.

The stack generates an overflow signal (PC_O) when the stack is
overflowed and an empty signal (PC_E) when no locations in the stack
are occupied.  PC_O and PC_E are stored in the @33[SSTAT] register.

The PC stack is monitored by a stack watchdog, which will pop up a
message if the stack is overflowed.

N.B.  Editing stack counts and stack values can dramatically change
execution of user code.  Use with caution.  Also note that the EZ-ICE
takes up one stack location when in emulator space, so entering a
stack count of 0x10 will cause a stack overflow.  All locations are
available when the part is in user space (running).

.107
Status Stack
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This is the display of the Status Stack.  The Status Stack is 7
locations deep for the 2101 and 2105. It is 9 locations deep for the
2111.  It contains the pushed contents of the astat, mstat, and imask
registers.

The display shows the individual values of astat, mstat and imask, as
well as their combined value which is actually pushed onto the stack.
Also shown is the current count of valid stack entries.  Valid stack
entries are writable (except combined field) as is the stack count.

N.B.  Editing stack counts and stack values can dramatically change
execution of user code.  Use with caution.  Also note that the EZ-ICE
takes up one stack location when in emulator space, so entering a
stack count of 7 will cause a stack overflow.  All locations are
available when the part is in user space (running).

See @33[Status registers] and @35[Interrupt Registers]

.108
Counter Stack
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This is the display of the Counter Stack.  The Counter Stack
is 4 locations deep and 14 bits wide.  Also displayed is the number of
valid stack locations.  Valid stack entries are writable as is the
stack count.

The stack generates an overflow signal (LS_O) when the stack is
overflowed and an empty signal (LS_E) when no locations in the stack
are occupied.  LS_O and LS_E are stored in the @33[SSTAT] register.

N.B.  Editing stack counts and stack values can dramatically change
execution of user code.  Use with caution.  The EZ-ICE does not use
any locations of this stack so all four are always available.

.110
PM Symbol Display
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This is the display of all @52[Program Memory] (PM) symbols.  
The symbols can be displayed in either alphabetical or
numeric order, according to the selection from the
@1[menu bar].  The display shows the symbolic name
and the address of the symbol.  Symbols are loaded into the EZ-ICE via
the @96[Load Executable File] and  @97[Load Symbol File] function.

At present, only global and static symbols are recognized.

See also @114[Delete Symbols], and @115[Data Memory (DM) Symbol Display]. 

.114
Delete Symbols
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function will delete all symbols from the EZ-ICE.

See also @115[Data Memory (DM) Symbol Display],and @110[Program Memory (PM) Symbol Display].

.115
DM Symbol Display
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This is the display of all @56[Data Memory] (DM) symbols.  
The symbols can be displayed in either alphabetical or
numeric order, according to the selection from the
@1[menu bar].  The display shows the symbolic name
and the address of the symbol.  Symbols are loaded into the EZ-ICE via
the @96[Load Executable File] and @97[Load Symbol File] function.

At present, only global and static symbols are recognized.

See also @114[Delete Symbols], and @110[Program Memory (PM) Symbol Display].

.117
Step
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to single step the EZ-ICE.  A single step will
take from 1 to 16 clock cycles, due to memory wait states etc..  The
display is automatically updated after each step.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar], via the @178[<F10>] key, or
from the @77[Command Window] by typing "s".

N.B. There are a number of restrictions concerning the step function.
Please see the EZ-ICE users manual, and your release note for more
information.

See also the @118[Step 'n' Times] and @119[Run] commands.

.118
Step 'n' Times
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to execute 'n' single steps of the EZ-ICE.  A
single step will take from 1 to 16 clock cycles, due to memory wait
states etc..  This function will halt upon reaching a breakpoint, or
pressing any key.  The display is automatically updated after each
step.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar], via the @178[<F10>] key, or
from the @77[Command Window] by typing "s N" (Where N is the number of steps).

When this function is activated a pop-up will appear requesting the
number of steps to execute, except when called from the Command
Window.

See also the @117[Step] and @119[Run] commands.

.119
Run
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to begin continuous execution of the EZ-ICE.  The 
EZ-ICE will continue executing until one of the following occur:

             - An instruction at which a breakpoint is set was about to be
                 executed (i.e..e.: PC = <breakpoint address>).

             - A keystroke has been detected.  

             - An error has been detected.

This function is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar], via the @178[<F4>] key, or from
the @77[Command Window] by typing "run".

When the DSP is running, the user can stop execution by pressing the
space bar, or exit to DOS by pressing 'X' (shift x).  See the EZ-ICE
users manual or your release note for more information.

See also the @118[Step 'n' Times] and @117[Step] commands.

.165
Overlay Memory Enable
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

Overlay memory consists of 8K of 24 bit words on your EZ-ICE board.  It can be 
configured as @52[Program Memory (PM)], @56[Data Memory (DM)], or both,
depending on the position of jumper JP1.  This function will enable the use of 
overlay memory if the jumper is set.  For more information about overlay memory, 
consult the ADSP-2101 EZ-ICE manual.

Notes: This function will have no effect if overlay memory has been disabled 
       via hardware (no jumper on JP1).

       The EZ-ICE cannot detect the position of jumper JP1.  It is the users 
       responsibility to ensure the jumper is properly set if they wish to 
       utilize overlay memory.

See also @166[Overlay memory disable], and @167[Overlay memory status].

.166
Overlay Memory Disable
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]
This function will disable use of the EZ-ICE's overlay memory.

Note:  This function will have no effect if overlay memory has been disabled 
       via hardware (no jumper on JP1).

See also @165[Overlay memory enable], and @167[Overlay memory status].

.167
Overlay Memory Status
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]
This function will return the status of the EZ-ICE's overlay memory.

Note:  This function's output is meaningless if overlay memory has been disabled 
       via hardware (no jumper on JP1).

See also @165[Overlay memory enable], and @166[Overlay memory disable].

.169
Set/Clear Breakpoint
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to set or clear a breakpoint.  A breakpoint is an address
at which the EZ-ICE will stop executing immediately prior to the execution 
of the specified address.  

This function is only available from the @52[Program Memory] (PM) window in the
disassembled format.  Breakpoints are displayed in the disassembled PM
window.  They are indicated by the highlighting of the access field at
the breakpoint address.  Use of this function will toggle the state of
a breakpoint at the address at which the cursor is present; if a
breakpoint is not set at that address, one will be established; if a
breakpoint is set at that address, it will be cleared.

Note that once a breakpoint is "hit", it remains active. 

This function is normally invoked from the @1[menu bar], the @178[<F9>] key,
or by double clicking with the mouse on the instruction field in the PM window.

See also @171[Display Bkpt] and @172[Clear All Breakpoints].  

.171
Display Breakpoints
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to display breakpoints.  When invoked, this function 
produces a pop-up window on the display.  The display contains the symbol name
(if any) or address of each breakpoint set in memory.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  Breaks can also be viewed
in the @85[Command Output Window], by typing "break" in the @77[Command Window].

See also @169[Set/Clear Breakpoint] and @172[Clear All Breakpoints].  
.172
Clear All Breakpoints
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

This function is used to clear all breakpoints in the EZ-ICE.  A
pop-up window will ask you to confirm before any action is taken.

This function is invoked from the @1[menu bar].  Breakpoints can also 
be cleared by typing "bd all" in the @77[Command Window].  No confirmation is required when 
deleting from the Command Window.

See also @169[Set/Clear Breakpoint] and @171[Display Breakpoints].  

.176
Help on Help
     @0[Return to EZ-ICE]         @-1[Previous Screen]         @179[General Info]

The on-line help system of the ADSP-2100 Family EZ-ICEs is designed to be 
easy to understand and simple to use.  The help system is also context 
sensitive.  This means that the help system is sensitive to what you are doing,
or trying to do. 

You can get help at any time by pressing the <F1> key.  The screen will then
display a help message pertinent to whatever window you were working in.  Once
the help system is entered, you can choose to remain in the help system or
return to the EZ-ICE.  

At the top of the screen, and in the ASCII of many help screens, are words that 
are, typically, in a different color.  These are called keywords and provide an
easy and efficient means of moving throughout the help system.  Selecting these
keywords, either by clicking on them with the mouse or using the arrow keys to 
choose one and pressing <ENTER>, will switch from the current help screen to 
a help screen containing help on your chosen keyword.  Pressing the <ESC> key 
anywhere in the help system will always return you to the EZ-ICE.  

Note: The arrow keys will scroll the help screens where appropriate.

